CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^.*symbol 'first' is unknown.*$
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
Verifies that two range-based fors in the same function with
different element types both correctly deduce the loop variable
type from their respective iterators.

Per [stmt.ranged]: each range-based for has its own
`__range`/`__begin`/`__end` auxiliaries.  CBMC's lowering
materialises these as named auxiliary symbols; if multiple
range-fors share the same name, the second loop reuses the
first's symbol via lookup, mis-deducing its loop variable.

Without the fix, the second loop's `entry` was auto-deduced as
`elem_t` (the first loop's element type) instead of `pair_t`,
so `entry.first` failed name lookup with the spurious
"symbol 'first' is unknown" diagnostic.

Three files in CBMC's own source — `irep.cpp`, `lispirep.cpp`,
`merge_irep.cpp` — hit this pattern.
